  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Platizio (Next.js 16 / React 19)\n\nScope note first: this is a **fully static content site** — no database, no API routes (`src/app/**/route.ts` doesn't exist), no `fetch`/`axios`/ORM calls anywhere. Both dynamic routes (`insights/[slug]`, `products/[slug]`) use `generateStaticParams` + `dynamicParams = false`, so every page is pre-rendered at build time from in-memory TS objects. **Classic N+1 query patterns don't apply here** — there's no query layer to fan out. I looked for the closest analog (repeated linear scans in render) and folded that into \"redundant computation\" below instead of forcing a finding that doesn't fit.\n\nThe real cost centers are the animation layer (three.js hero, Framer Motion/`motion`, Lenis) and a handful of un-memoized render-time computations.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Biggest win: `MarketConstellation.tsx` runs an unthrottled CPU vertex loop every frame\n\n`src/components/three/MarketConstellation.tsx:60-69` — `Terrain`'s `useFrame` recomputes `waveY()` (3 sin/cos pairs) for all `110 × 55 = 6,050` points, on the CPU, every animation frame, for as long as the hero is mounted:\n\n```tsx\nuseFrame((state) => {\n  if (reduced) return;\n  const t = state.clock.getElapsedTime();\n  const attr = geometry.getAttribute(\"position\") as THREE.BufferAttribute;\n  const arr = attr.array as Float32Array;\n  for (let i = 0; i < baseX.length; i++) {\n    arr[i * 3 + 1] = waveY(baseX[i], baseZ[i], t); // ~5 trig calls × 6050 pts × 60fps\n  }\n  attr.needsUpdate = true;\n});\n```\n\nThat's ~1.8M trig evaluations/sec on the main thread, sustained indefinitely (not \"leaked\" memory, but a continuous CPU/battery drain — worst on low-end mobile where the hero is likely the LCP element). Two fixes, cheapest first:\n\n**Quick fix — frame-skip (halves the cost, no visual difference at 24fps update rate):**\n```tsx\nconst frame = useRef(0);\nuseFrame((state) => {\n  if (reduced) return;\n  if (++frame.current % 2 !== 0) return; // update terrain at ~30fps instead of 60fps\n  const t = state.clock.getElapsedTime();\n  ...\n});\n```\n\n**Proper fix — move displacement into a vertex shader** so the GPU does the per-vertex work instead of JS touching a `Float32Array` every frame:\n```tsx\nconst material = useMemo(() => new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n  uniforms: { uTime: { value: 0 } },\n  vertexShader: /* glsl */ `\n    uniform float uTime;\n    void main() {\n      vec3 p = position;\n      p.y = 0.42 * sin(p.x * 0.32 + uTime * 0.35) * cos(p.z * 0.45 - uTime * 0.22)\n           + 0.22 * sin(p.x * 0.78 + p.z * 0.52 - uTime * 0.16)\n           + 0.1  * cos(p.z * 1.15 + p.x * 0.12 + uTime * 0.28);\n      gl_PointSize = 3.0;\n      gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(p, 1.0);\n    }`,\n  fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `void main() { gl_FragColor = vec4(0.56, 0.48, 1.0, 0.7); }`,\n  transparent: true,\n}), []);\nuseFrame((state) => { material.uniforms.uTime.value = state.clock.getElapsedTime(); });\n```\nThis removes the JS loop entirely — one uniform update per frame instead of 6,050 array writes.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders\n\n**`src/components/Nav.tsx:23-28`** — unthrottled native `scroll` listener fires on every scroll tick:\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const onScroll = () => setScrolled(window.scrollY > 48);\n  onScroll();\n  window.addEventListener(\"scroll\", onScroll, { passive: true });\n  return () => window.removeEventListener(\"scroll\", onScroll);\n}, []);\n```\nSince `scrolled` is a boolean, React bails out of re-rendering when the value doesn't change (fine), but the handler itself still runs on *every* scroll event, and the page already runs Lenis's own `requestAnimationFrame` loop (`SmoothScroll.tsx`). Piggyback on that instead of adding a second, independent scroll listener:\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  let raf = 0;\n  const check = () => {\n    setScrolled(window.scrollY > 48);\n    raf = requestAnimationFrame(check);\n  };\n  raf = requestAnimationFrame(check);\n  return () => cancelAnimationFrame(raf);\n}, []);\n```\nor better, subscribe to Lenis's own `scroll` event (it already exposes one) instead of polling `window.scrollY` from two separate places.\n\n**`src/components/pages/ArticlePage.tsx:24-30`** — `buildToc(body)`, `readingMinutes(body)`, and the `ARTICLES_SORTED.filter(...)` \"related articles\" scan all run unmemoized on every render:\n```tsx\nconst toc = buildToc(body);                 // O(n) over blocks, every render\nconst minutes = readingMinutes(body);        // O(words) over all blocks, every render\nconst related = ARTICLES_SORTED.filter(...); // scan, every render\n```\n`body` and `article` are stable props (server-passed, static content), so wrap these:\n```tsx\nconst toc = useMemo(() => buildToc(body), [body]);\nconst minutes = useMemo(() => readingMinutes(body), [body]);\nconst related = useMemo(\n  () => ARTICLES_SORTED.filter(a => a.slug !== article.slug && a.category === article.category).slice(0, 2),\n  [article.slug, article.category],\n);\n```\nLow-frequency component (no re-render triggers here today), so impact is small now — but it's dead-cheap to fix and prevents it from becoming a real cost if these pages ever gain client-side state (e.g. a \"reading progress\" indicator, which is a very likely future addition to a long-form article page).\n\n**`src/components/sections/Journey.tsx:61-65`** — `useMotionValueEvent(scrollYProgress, \"change\", ...)` calls `setActive` on every scroll-progress tick across a ~300vh scrollytelling track. It's bounded to 3 values (`STEPS.length`) so React bails most of the time, but consider gating the `setActive` call so it only fires when the computed index actually changes:\n```tsx\nuseMotionValueEvent(scrollYProgress, \"change\", (v) => {\n  const next = Math.max(0, Math.min(STEPS.length - 1, Math.floor(v * STEPS.length)));\n  setActive((prev) => (prev === next ? prev : next));\n});\n```\n(functionally equivalent to today given React's bailout, but makes the intent explicit and avoids the setState call entirely on no-op frames).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Redundant computation\n\n**`src/components/sections/Trust.tsx:7-23`** and **`src/components/pages/AboutPage.tsx:82-97`** both build tick-mark SVG arrays with `Array.from(...).map(() => Math.cos/sin(...))` **inline in the component body**, so they're recomputed on every render even though the inputs never change:\n```tsx\nfunction ticks(count: number, r1: number, r2: number, strokeWidth: number) {\n  return Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => { /* trig */ });\n}\n```\nThese components don't currently re-render often, so the practical cost is near-zero today — but it's a one-line fix to make it free forever. Hoist to module scope (or `useMemo` keyed on the static args) since the geometry is fully deterministic:\n```tsx\n// module scope — computed once per bundle load, not per render\nconst AMFI_TICKS = ticks(28, 26.5, 29.5, 0.75);\nconst CENTER_TICKS = ticks(4, 12.5, 22.5, 1.25);\n```\nFor `AboutPage.tsx`'s `CompassSeal`, since there are only two `variant` values, precompute both at module scope and index into them rather than recomputing per render:\n```tsx\nconst TICKS_BY_VARIANT = {\n  amfi: buildTicks(24, 23.5),\n  sebi: buildTicks(8, 17),\n} as const;\n```\n\n**`src/components/sections/Products.tsx:16-51`** duplicates the `PRODUCTS` catalog (name/slug/tag/description) that already lives in `src/lib/products.tsx` (`hero.intro` ≈ the same copy). Two hand-maintained copies of the same content means the homepage teaser can silently drift from the product detail pages, and it's shipping duplicate string literals in the client bundle. Derive the homepage row data from the single source of truth instead:\n```tsx\nimport { PRODUCTS as PRODUCT_DETAILS } from \"@/lib/products\";\nconst PRODUCTS = PRODUCT_DETAILS.map(p => ({ slug: p.slug, name: p.name, tag: p.tag, description: p.hero.intro }));\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks\n\nGood news: I checked every `useEffect`/`useFrame`/listener across the client components (`Nav`, `Preloader`, `SmoothScroll`, `MarketConstellation`, `IntroProvider`) and **all of them clean up correctly** — `removeEventListener`, `cancelAnimationFrame`, `lenis.destroy()`, and `geometry.dispose()` are all present and paired with their setup. No `setInterval` without a matching `clearInterval`, no observers left dangling (there are none in the codebase at all — no `IntersectionObserver`/`ResizeObserver`/`MutationObserver` usage).\n\nThe one thing worth flagging isn't a leak in the classic sense but has leak-like symptoms: `MarketConstellation`'s per-frame CPU loop (finding #1) runs for the entire lifetime of the Hero being mounted, so on a page that stays open a long time (e.g. a background tab, or a user reading the hero copy for minutes), it's continuously burning CPU/battery rather than idling — the frame-skip or shader fix above addresses this directly. `frameloop=\"always\"` (`MarketConstellation.tsx:182`) is also correctly switched to `\"demand\"` under `prefers-reduced-motion`, but note it stays `\"always\"` for everyone else regardless of tab visibility — Page Visibility isn't checked, so an inactive/background tab keeps rendering. Consider pausing the Canvas when `document.visibilityState !== \"visible\"`.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Caching\n\nThe site already does the right thing at the framework level:\n- `generateStaticParams` + `dynamicParams = false` on both dynamic routes → full SSG, served from CDN/cache with zero runtime work.\n- `next/font/google` (Fraunces, DM Sans) → self-hosted + cached, no runtime font-fetch waterfall.\n- `MarketConstellation` is `next/dynamic(..., { ssr: false })` → correctly code-split out of the initial bundle so the ~three.js/`@react-three/fiber` payload doesn't block first paint.\n- `next/image` with `fill`/`sizes` is used correctly for the About page's team photos and founder image — no raw `<img>` tags anywhere in the codebase.\n\nNothing here needs a data cache (there's no data layer to cache). The only caching-adjacent suggestion: `ARTICLES_SORTED` (`src/lib/articles.ts:167`) and `PRODUCT_MAP` (`src/lib/products.tsx:517`) are already computed once at module scope rather than per-request — good pattern, worth keeping as the content set grows instead of resorting/rebuilding inline in components.\n\n---\n\n### Priority order\n1. `MarketConstellation` per-frame CPU loop — real, measurable cost on every homepage/about-page load, worst on mobile.\n2. `Nav` scroll listener → rAF-throttle (cheap fix, removes a redundant timing source alongside Lenis).\n3. `Trust`/`AboutPage` tick arrays → hoist to module scope (trivial, zero-risk).\n4. `ArticlePage` memoization + dedup `Products.tsx` catalog — maintainability/future-proofing more than current perf, but cheap to do while in the area.\n",
